#!/usr/bin/env python3
# burrow-gen: the code burrow generates rather than writes by hand. There are two
# subcommands and they have nothing in common except that neither belongs in a
# compiler.
#
#     burrow-gen reflect      type descriptors for structs you already have
#     burrow-gen amalgamate   the whole library as one burrow.c and one burrow.h
#
# REFLECT
#
# The X macro DSL in burrow/declare.h is the default way to get a descriptor,
# and it needs no tool and no build step. It also needs you to write your struct
# as a field list, which is fine for new code and is not going to happen to a
# hundred thousand lines of existing C. This reads the structs you already have
# and emits the same descriptors the DSL would have.
#
#     /* burrow:reflect */
#     typedef struct {
#         Int X     BURROW_TAG("json:\"x\"");
#         Int Y     BURROW_TAG("json:\"y\"");
#         Str Label BURROW_TAG("json:\"label,omitempty\"");
#     } Point;
#
#     burrow-gen reflect shapes.h -o shapes_gen.c -Iinclude
#
# Neither path is deprecated and neither is preferred. The DSL is the default
# because of the no build step promise, and this exists because a large existing
# codebase will not restructure its headers to get %v to work.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COMPUTE
#
# It does not compute offsets, sizes or alignments. It emits offsetof, sizeof
# and _Alignof and lets the compiler that is actually building for the target
# work them out. That is not laziness, it is the only correct answer: a
# generator that computed 12 for an offset would be computing it for whatever
# machine the generator ran on, and the whole point of a portable library is
# that the answer is different on some of them. The generator's job is to find
# out which fields exist and what they are called, and nothing else.
#
# WHAT IT NEEDS
#
# Python 3 and a libclang shared library, which you already have if you compile
# C with clang. There is no pip install: the binding is about a hundred lines of
# ctypes at the bottom of this file, because a code generator that makes you
# build a virtualenv first is a code generator people work around.
#
# libclang is not needed to build burrow, to use the DSL, or to compile what
# this emits. It is needed to run this, once, when your structs change.
#
# AMALGAMATE
#
#     burrow-gen amalgamate --out dist --source-id "$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
#
# writes dist/burrow.c, dist/burrow.h and dist/burrow-manifest.json. The two C
# files are the whole library, the way SQLite ships: add burrow.c to a build,
# include burrow.h, and there is nothing else to install. burrow.h is the public
# headers in dependency order. burrow.c is every private header and then every
# source file, each behind a marker and a #line so that errors and debuggers
# name the file the code really lives in.
#
# It is generated and never edited, and it is not clever. Nothing is rewritten
# except include lines, which become comments so the line numbers stay right. A
# source file's own macros are undefined at the end of that file, because two
# files are allowed to mean different things by STACK_FLOOR. What it cannot fix
# is two files with the same static name, and tools/check-statics.sh is what
# keeps those out of the tree. Running it twice on the same tree gives the same
# bytes, and CI checks that. It needs Python 3 and nothing else.
#
# docs/design/15-build-deploy.md section 2 is the design, including the options
# that are not here yet: choosing packages, choosing targets, and a prefix.
#
